      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <section id="section-5" class="section">
        <h2>Aerosol Shave Foam Chemistry and Formulation Dynamics</h2>
        <p>Understanding aerosol shave foam chemistry requires analyzing the molecular interaction between fatty acid soaps and water. A typical shave foam relies on a combination of stearic, myristic, and palmitic acids neutralized in situ with triethanolamine (TEA) or potassium hydroxide. These long-chain carboxylate soaps self-assemble into complex micellar structures. When combined with fatty alcohols, they form a crystalline gel network that traps water molecules within its layers. This gel network increases the mechanical strength of the bubble walls, providing the necessary cushion and lubrication to protect the skin during shaving. Humectants, such as glycerin or propylene glycol, are added to prevent the foam from drying out on the skin surface before the shaving process is complete.</p>
        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--amber); background: #fffbeb; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;"><strong style="color: var(--amber); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Rule of Thumb</strong>The molar ratio of fatty acids to the neutralizing base must be carefully balanced. A slight excess of unreacted fatty acid (superfatting) helps stabilize the gel network and improves post-shave skin feel, whereas excess free amine or alkali destabilizes the emulsion and causes skin irritation.</div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <section id="section-6" class="section">
        <h2>Key Parameters for a Stable Foam Aerosol Formulation</h2>
        <p>Achieving a stable foam aerosol formulation requires meticulous optimization of several physical-chemical parameters. First, the emulsion must be stable at elevated temperatures to prevent phase separation within the canister, which would lead to inconsistent gassing and variable foam delivery. Second, the propellant blend must be selected to provide a constant vapor pressure throughout the life of the product, ensuring the final spray behaves the same from the first actuation to the last. Third, the internal container surface must be compatible with the aqueous concentrate. Aqueous foams require corrosion-resistant tinplate or lined aluminum cans to prevent chemical oxidation and rust. For market data and general aerosol packaging standards, consult the <a href="https://aerosol.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">European Aerosol Federation (FEA)</a>.</p>
        <p>Formulators must also verify the compatibility of the valve cup gasket. Liquefied propellants, particularly DME, can cause certain rubber elastomers to swell, leading to valve leakage or actuator failure. EPDM or specialized neoprene gaskets are commonly selected for DME-based formulations to maintain seal integrity over time. Extensive stability trials, including freeze-thaw cycles and high-temperature storage testing (45°C for 3 months), are mandatory to validate the long-term physical and chemical stability of the aerosol foam system before mass manufacturing.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ Section -->
