            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What is foam aerosol engineering?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Foam aerosol engineering is the technical discipline of designing pressurized systems that produce stable, structured foam upon actuation. It requires balancing surfactant types, emulsion stability, and propellant thermodynamics to control bubble size, expansion ratio, and foam density.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How does surfactant selection affect aerosol foam stability?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Surfactant selection determines the surface tension reduction and the mechanical strength of the bubble walls. Anionic soaps provide rapid foaming and rich lather, while nonionic co-surfactants pack tightly between ionic heads to stabilize the liquid lamellae against drainage.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What is foam drain rate testing?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Foam drain rate testing is a laboratory method used to measure the time required for liquid to drain from the foam structure. It is typically expressed as the foam half-life, which indicates how long the foam remains stable before gravity and capillary pressure trigger bubble collapse.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Why is foam density a critical parameter in aerosol products?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Foam density dictates the texture, spreadability, and cushion of the dispensed foam. Lower density foams are light and break down quickly, which is ideal for sanitizers, while higher density foams provide a rich lather suitable for shaving and styling applications.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What role do liquefied gas propellants play in foam generation?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Liquefied gas propellants dissolve in the liquid concentrate under pressure and flash boil upon actuation. This sudden expansion disperses the surfactant solution into millions of micro-bubbles, forming the gas phase of the resulting foam matrix.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="padding-bottom: 8px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How does container compatibility impact stable foam aerosol formulations?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Since foam concentrates are typically water-based, they pose a high risk of container corrosion. Formulators must select lined aluminum or epoxy-coated tinplate cans to prevent oxidation, chemical reactions, and propellant leakage over the shelf life of the product.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

